  "Number": 45,
  "Question": "The lengths of two line segments are \\(a\\) units and \\(b\\) units respectively. Then the correct relation between them is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\gt\\sqrt{ab}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\lt\\sqrt{ab}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}=\\sqrt{ab}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\le\\sqrt{ab}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\ge\\sqrt{ab}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "A strict inequality cannot always hold, because the two segments may have equal lengths. To find the relation that always holds, use a square, which is nonnegative. Since lengths satisfy \\(a,b\\ge0\\), their square roots are defined, and\n\\[\n0\\le(\\sqrt a-\\sqrt b)^2\n=a+b-2\\sqrt{ab}.\n\\]\nRearranging and dividing by \\(2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{a+b}{2}\\ge\\sqrt{ab}.\n\\]\nEquality holds exactly when \\(\\sqrt a=\\sqrt b\\), or \\(a=b\\); otherwise the inequality is strict. The correct relation is choice E.",
